module gunbc.language_subject_scope_scaffold


data scaffold_home_note: String = "WHY THIS LIVES AT THE PRODUCT LAYER RATHER THAN IN THE SUBJECT CARRIERS (cursor review 48122, P1). Twenty-four upstream subject carriers each repeated this same implementation essay verbatim. That was duplicated MUTABLE REASONING — one correction would have needed twenty-four edits — and worse, it embedded gunbc's own implementation policy inside modules whose entire job is to describe an independently governed upstream. A carrier for the C language should not explain this repository's scope-gate text scan; that is a fact about gunbc, not about ISO/IEC 9899. The one necessary ALIAS stays in each carrier because the mechanism genuinely requires it there; the EXPLANATION lives here once. AND THE PER-CARRIER POINTER IS NOW GONE TOO, which is the second half of the same lesson (claude review 48532). The first fix left each carrier with a one-line note saying 'the shared explanation lives once at gunbc.language_subject_scope_scaffold' — twenty-four byte-identical copies of a pointer whose entire content was that the explanation is not repeated. That is still a parallel representation, just a smaller one, and it was worse than inert: every copy named this module, so a rename would have left twenty-four stale citations of exactly the class review kept catching on that PR. Nothing mechanical referenced the marker — the placement gate and cover witness read 'data extdeps_model_scope:' and never the note — so removing it cost no enforcement. A reader who wants to know why a carrier declares its scope under a unique name and binds the mandated name to it finds the answer here, at the one place that can be kept true."

data mandated_scope_name_scaffold: String = "DECLARED SCAFFOLD, with its dissolution triggers — why every language subject carrier authors its scope under a unique name and then binds the mandated name to it. The authored value is the unique one; 'extdeps_model_scope' is a BINDING to that same value, not a second authority, and it cannot drift from its source because it IS its source. It exists because two independent mechanisms collide. (1) gunbc.extdeps_scope_frontier's carrier_content_declares_scope text-scans every scope carrier for the literal 'data extdeps_model_scope:', so that name is mandatory in every carrier. (2) The resolver offers no way to tell two same-named imports apart: MEASURED 2026-08-04 against the real binary, a qualified reference fails with 'undefined variable', module projection after an import fails the same way, no import aliasing exists in the grammar, and — the reason this is not merely inconvenient — importing one name from two modules does NOT refuse, it SILENTLY BINDS THE LAST ONE with exit 0. A registry importing the mandated name from every carrier would therefore ground all but one target in the WRONG upstream scope and, because registration_subject derives the subject from its grounding, report the wrong subject for each: green and materially false. The unique name is what keeps that unwritable. DISSOLVE-ON, either trigger suffices: the resolver gains import aliasing or module projection, at which point the registry imports the mandated name directly and every binding deletes; OR the v2.lens.mandatory_tag region promotion retires the carrier_content_declares_scope text scan, at which point the mandated name is no longer required and the unique names stand alone. This is a scaffold pinned to a scaffold — carrier_content_declares_scope calls itself a declared scaffold in its own note — and they retire together. The resolver defect itself is corpus-wide and is tracked as its own lane; it is not a language-support fact."

data subject_singleton_name_collision_note: String = "SUBJECT SINGLETON NAMES MUST NOT COLLIDE WITH std.languages CATALOGUE ROWS, and five of them did (CI run 30982390705, 2026-08-05). The D0 carriers minted go_language, python_language, rust_language, typescript_language and yaml_format as their subject singletons — names dag/std/languages.dag already owns for its Language and ConfigFormat catalogue rows. The tell was a compile failure a long way from the cause: dag/test/claim/config_record_emit_test.dag reads yaml_format.record via a BARE cross-module reference and reported undefined variable, because two declarations of that name were reachable in one assembled closure and neither won. HOW MUCH OF THIS WAS LUCK IS THE POINT: only yaml_format had a bare-reference consumer, so only yaml_format redded. The other four were latent and would have surfaced whenever some future closure resolved them bare — a defect landed, green, waiting for an unrelated change to detonate it. Renamed to the ..._subject form, which also says what the declaration IS rather than repeating the catalogue row name. THE DEEPER CAUSE is the measured resolver defect recorded in mandated_scope_name_scaffold: the resolver has no aliasing, no module projection, and silently binds the last of two same-named imports. Under that behaviour a name collision is not a loud redeclaration error but an unpredictable bind, so the corpus cannot rely on collisions announcing themselves. Until the containment-tree naming authority lands, a new subject carrier must check its singleton name against the whole corpus before landing; the check is a grep and the failure mode it prevents is a silent wrong bind in another module."
